        ":question/id": "19d2f443-e38d-5ded-a277-4288cfd36f31",
        ":question/problem": "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=|x|.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=|x+3|-2?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=|x+3|-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=|x|.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=|x+3|.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-3,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=|x|$, we have that $|a|=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ Substituting $x=a-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =|a-3+3|-2 \\\\  & =|a|-2 \\\\  & =b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-3,b-2)$ must lie on $y=|x+3|-2.$",
