        ":question/problem": "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=3^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3^{x/2}-2?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3^{x/2}-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=3^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=3^{x/2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=3^{x/2}-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(2a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=3^{x}$, we have that $3^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3^{x/2}-2.$ Substituting $x=2a$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=3^{\\frac{2a}{2}}-2 \\\\ &=3^{a}-2 \\\\ &=b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2a,b-2)$ must lie on $y=3^{x/2}-2.$",
